-- CISSP Domain 6 (Security Assessment and Testing) expansion: adds flashcards ci-f-1400..1454 (core), ci-a-460..471 (acronym), ci-y-290..298 (feynman), and questions ci-q-1400..1463, covering 6.1 designing/validating assessment and test strategies (scope statements, internal vs third-party assessors, ROE, assessment vs audit vs review), 6.2 control testing (credentialed scanning, fuzzing, IAST, red/blue/purple teams, misuse-case testing, code coverage, 800-53A method triangulation, control-testing log review), 6.3 collecting security process data (account review data, backup verification data, training completion data, DR test data, MTTD/MTTR, GRC evidence sources), 6.4 analyzing test output and generating reports (CVSS severity, business-context risk rating, POA&M, executive vs technical reporting, deduplication, false negatives, re-testing to closure), and 6.5 conducting/facilitating security audits (right-to-audit clauses, CUECs, bridge letters, QSA, continuous vs periodic auditing, sampling, audit opinions, SSAE 18/ISAE 3402, auditor independence).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 6.1 design and validate assessment/test strategies: core
('ci-f-1400','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is a security assessment scope statement, and why must it be defined before testing begins?$$,$$A document specifying exactly which systems, networks, applications, time period, and assessment types are in bounds (and explicitly what is excluded). Defining it upfront prevents unauthorized scope creep, protects out-of-scope production systems from disruption, and gives everyone a shared, unambiguous basis for what the assessment's conclusions do and don't cover.$$),
('ci-f-1401','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the difference between an assessment, an audit, and a review as three distinct evaluation activities?$$,$$An assessment broadly evaluates security posture using a mix of techniques (scans, interviews, testing) without necessarily following a fixed external standard. An audit is a formal, independent, evidence-based verification against a specific standard or criteria, often producing a certifiable opinion. A review is a lighter-weight, less formal periodic check (e.g., an access review) that need not be fully independent.$$),
('ci-f-1402','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What factors should be weighed when deciding whether to use an internal team or a third-party firm for a security assessment?$$,$$Objectivity/independence (a third party has no internal stake in the outcome), specialized expertise or tooling the internal team may lack, cost, the internal team's existing knowledge of the environment, whether a regulator or contract mandates independent assessment, and whether internal staff have the bandwidth.$$),
('ci-f-1403','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is a Rules of Engagement (ROE) document, and what does it typically define for a security test?$$,$$The document that sets the authorized boundaries of a test: in-scope and explicitly out-of-scope systems, permitted testing windows and techniques, emergency stop/escalation contacts, and the written authorization confirming management accepted the operational risk of testing.$$),
('ci-f-1404','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$Why is written authorization essential before any active security test begins?$$,$$It legally protects the tester (sometimes called a "get out of jail free" letter), prevents the activity from being mistaken for a real attack by defenders or law enforcement, and provides documented proof that an accountable owner accepted the risk of the test occurring.$$),
('ci-f-1405','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What inputs typically drive which assets or areas get prioritized when planning what to assess first?$$,$$Asset criticality and business impact, applicable regulatory or contractual requirements, the current threat landscape and any recent incidents, findings and gaps carried over from prior assessments, and the budget and resources actually available.$$),
('ci-f-1406','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the purpose of defining "success criteria" or objectives in an assessment's test plan?$$,$$It states the concrete question the assessment is meant to answer (e.g., "can an external attacker reach the cardholder data environment?"), giving the exercise a measurable target rather than leaving it as an open-ended, unfocused activity whose results are hard to judge as complete.$$),
('ci-f-1407','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$Why should the people planning or conducting an assessment be independent of the system owners whose environment is being assessed?$$,$$Independence removes conflict of interest and self-review bias, produces more objective and credible findings, and is frequently a hard requirement of compliance frameworks and contracts, since an assessor evaluating their own work cannot be trusted to report unfavorable results.$$),
('ci-f-1408','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is an assessment cadence policy, and why does it matter for a testing strategy?$$,$$A documented schedule of how often each assessment type recurs (e.g., quarterly vulnerability scans, annual penetration tests). It matters because risk changes continuously, so a one-time assessment only proves a point-in-time snapshot rather than sustained assurance.$$),
('ci-f-1409','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What stakeholder sign-off is typically required before a security assessment can proceed?$$,$$Authorization from the system or data owner whose environment will be tested, and often from senior management or legal, confirming they accept the defined scope, timing, and any operational risk the testing activity could introduce.$$),
-- 6.1 design and validate assessment/test strategies: acronym
('ci-a-460','cissp','acronym',6,'6.1',$$ROE$$,$$Rules of Engagement - the document defining a test's authorized scope, timing, permitted techniques, and escalation contacts before it begins.$$),
('ci-a-461','cissp','acronym',6,'6.1',$$SOW$$,$$Statement of Work - a contractual document defining the deliverables, scope, schedule, and cost of an assessment engagement, especially with a third-party assessor.$$),
-- 6.1 design and validate assessment/test strategies: feynman
('ci-y-290','cissp','feynman',6,'6.1',$$Explain to a new security hire why a written scope statement and rules of engagement must exist before any penetration test starts.$$,$$Model answer: Without a written scope and ROE, everyone involved is exposed. The tester has no legal protection - what they're doing could be indistinguishable from a real crime, and without explicit written authorization from someone with the authority to grant it, they could face criminal exposure even if their intentions were good. The organization is exposed too: without a defined scope, a tester could accidentally touch a fragile production system nobody meant to include, causing an outage. And defenders monitoring the network have no way to know an authorized test is even happening, so they might burn real incident-response time chasing what turns out to be sanctioned activity. The ROE fixes all of this by writing down, in advance, exactly what's in bounds, when testing can happen, who to call if something goes wrong, and who signed off accepting the risk - turning an ambiguous, risky activity into a controlled, accountable one.$$),
('ci-y-291','cissp','feynman',6,'6.1',$$Explain the trade-offs a CISO should weigh when deciding whether to use an internal team or hire a third-party firm for an annual security assessment.$$,$$Model answer: It comes down to independence versus context. An internal team already knows the environment intimately - the quirks of the legacy system, who to ask when something looks odd, which alerts are noise - so they can move faster and cheaper. But they also have a blind spot: it's hard to be truly objective about a system your own team built or maintains, and some regulators or customers explicitly won't accept a self-assessment as sufficient assurance precisely for that reason. A third-party firm brings fresh eyes, broader cross-industry experience, and credible independence that carries more weight externally - but they cost more, take time to ramp up on the environment, and may miss context an internal person would catch instantly. Many mature programs use both: internal teams for frequent, lower-stakes checks, and an independent third party for the assessments where objectivity and external credibility actually matter.$$),
-- 6.2 control testing: core
('ci-f-1410','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST), and how does it differ from SAST and DAST?$$,$$IAST places instrumentation (an agent) inside a running application during dynamic testing, combining source-code-level context with real runtime behavior. This lets it pinpoint the exact vulnerable line of code with fewer false positives than DAST alone, while still observing genuine execution paths that pure static analysis (SAST) never runs.$$),
('ci-f-1411','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is fuzz testing (fuzzing)?$$,$$An automated technique that feeds a target malformed, random, or unexpected input to try to trigger crashes, memory-safety errors, or unhandled exceptions, surfacing vulnerabilities that structured, expected-input testing would never exercise.$$),
('ci-f-1412','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the difference between a credentialed (authenticated) and an uncredentialed (unauthenticated) vulnerability scan?$$,$$A credentialed scan logs into the host with valid credentials to inspect internal configuration, installed software, and patch state in depth. An uncredentialed scan only probes what is visible from the network, similar to an outside attacker's view - faster and requiring no credential exposure, but far less thorough.$$),
('ci-f-1413','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the difference between a red team, a blue team, and a purple team exercise?$$,$$A red team simulates real-world attackers to test detection and response (offense). A blue team is the defenders detecting and responding to that activity. A purple team has red and blue actively collaborate and share techniques in real time, deliberately closing the loop to improve detection rather than only scoring a covert win or loss.$$),
('ci-f-1414','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is misuse case testing?$$,$$Testing that deliberately models how a malicious or careless user might abuse a feature - such as submitting a coupon code repeatedly or manipulating a price field - and verifies the system safely rejects or contains that abuse, rather than only testing the intended "happy path" use of the feature.$$),
('ci-f-1415','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What does code coverage measure in a testing context, and why does it matter for security testing specifically?$$,$$The percentage of source-code paths actually exercised by a test suite. It matters because a clean SAST or test result on a codebase with low coverage may simply reflect code that was never analyzed or executed, not code that was analyzed and found safe.$$),
('ci-f-1416','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$Why do assessors typically combine multiple NIST SP 800-53A assessment methods (Examine, Interview, Test) rather than relying on just one?$$,$$Triangulating evidence across methods increases assurance and can surface discrepancies - such as a policy document claiming one thing, staff describing a different actual practice, and hands-on testing revealing a third reality - that any single method alone would likely miss.$$),
('ci-f-1417','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is regression testing in a security control-testing context?$$,$$Re-testing a previously verified control or fix after a subsequent change, to confirm the control still functions correctly and that the change did not silently reintroduce a vulnerability that had already been remediated.$$),
('ci-f-1418','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is a false positive in vulnerability or control testing, and why does it matter operationally?$$,$$A finding reported as a weakness that, on investigation, is not actually present or exploitable in this environment. A high false-positive rate erodes analyst trust in the tooling and wastes remediation effort chasing non-issues, making tuning and triage an ongoing necessity.$$),
('ci-f-1419','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the value of reviewing change-management or configuration-approval logs as a control-testing technique?$$,$$It provides objective, after-the-fact evidence of whether a control - such as requiring documented approval before a production change - actually operated as designed, rather than relying only on an interview or the policy document that describes how it's supposed to work.$$),
-- 6.2 control testing: acronym
('ci-a-462','cissp','acronym',6,'6.2',$$IAST$$,$$Interactive Application Security Testing - instruments a running application during dynamic testing to combine source-level detail with real runtime behavior, pinpointing vulnerabilities more precisely than SAST or DAST alone.$$),
('ci-a-463','cissp','acronym',6,'6.2',$$PTES$$,$$Penetration Testing Execution Standard - a methodology defining the phases of a penetration test engagement, from pre-engagement scoping through reconnaissance, exploitation, and reporting.$$),
-- 6.2 control testing: feynman
('ci-y-292','cissp','feynman',6,'6.2',$$Explain to a junior analyst why a clean SAST scan result doesn't necessarily prove an application is secure.$$,$$Model answer: A SAST tool can only tell you about the code paths it actually analyzed and the vulnerability patterns it knows how to recognize. If large parts of the codebase have low code coverage in testing, or the tool simply doesn't understand a particular framework's idioms, a "clean" result might just mean those parts were never meaningfully examined - not that they're safe. SAST also can't see runtime-only issues like a misconfigured server header or an authentication bypass that only shows up when the application is actually running and being interacted with, which is exactly what DAST and IAST exist to catch. A clean static scan is one useful data point, not a guarantee - which is why mature programs combine SAST with dynamic testing, manual review, and coverage metrics rather than trusting any single tool's green checkmark.$$),
('ci-y-293','cissp','feynman',6,'6.2',$$Explain the difference between red team, blue team, and purple team exercises to someone unfamiliar with the terms.$$,$$Model answer: Think of it like a fire drill where one group secretly tries to start believable, controlled fires (the red team, playing the attacker) while another group's job is to notice the smoke and put it out before real damage happens (the blue team, the defenders). In a normal red-vs-blue exercise, the red team doesn't tell the blue team what it's doing - the whole point is to test whether blue can detect and respond to a surprise. A purple team exercise flips that secrecy off on purpose: red and blue sit together, red explains exactly what technique it's about to try, and blue explains in real time what they do or don't see on their end. Instead of a win-or-lose scorecard, the goal becomes a shared checklist of exactly which attack techniques the defenses currently catch and which ones slip through undetected - directly informing what detection rules need to be built next.$$),
-- 6.3 collect security process data: core
('ci-f-1420','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What data should an account management review collect to be effective?$$,$$The list of currently active accounts compared against the authorized personnel/role roster, last-login or last-activity dates, which accounts hold privileged access, and accounts flagged as stale or orphaned because no matching authorized user or business justification exists for them.$$),
('ci-f-1421','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What is a stale or orphaned account, and why is tracking its count important process data?$$,$$An account still enabled after the associated employee has left, changed roles, or no longer needs the access - representing unmonitored attack surface. The count of such accounts found during a review is itself a key metric showing how well deprovisioning is actually working.$$),
('ci-f-1422','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What data should be collected to verify that backups are genuinely working, beyond a job simply reporting "success"?$$,$$Results of periodic restore tests that prove data can actually be recovered, the percentage of backup jobs completing within their defined window, error and failure logs, and confirmation that restored data is complete and uncorrupted, not just that the backup process ran to completion.$$),
('ci-f-1423','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$Why is a "success" status on a backup job log insufficient by itself as security process data?$$,$$A job reporting success only confirms the backup process ran without error - it does not prove the resulting backup file is actually restorable. Only an executed restore test provides genuine evidence that the data can be recovered when needed.$$),
('ci-f-1424','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What security awareness training data should be collected and reported to management?$$,$$The completion rate/percentage of staff trained by their deadline, phishing simulation click and report rates, time elapsed since each person's last training, and how these figures trend across multiple training cycles.$$),
('ci-f-1425','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What is a phishing simulation click rate, and how is it typically used as process data?$$,$$The percentage of targeted users who clicked a simulated phishing link. It's tracked as a trend across successive campaigns to gauge whether the workforce's susceptibility to social engineering is improving, worsening, or plateauing over time.$$),
('ci-f-1426','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What disaster recovery (DR) test data should be collected to demonstrate a plan's effectiveness?$$,$$Whether the test met its target RTO and RPO, which systems successfully failed over and which did not, any issues or gaps encountered, and the date and type of test performed (e.g., tabletop versus full interruption).$$),
('ci-f-1427','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$Why is the frequency of DR testing itself (e.g., number of tests conducted per year) a meaningful metric, separate from whether the last test passed?$$,$$A DR plan that has not been recently validated provides false assurance regardless of how a past test went, since infrastructure and dependencies drift over time; testing cadence is tracked alongside pass/fail outcomes to catch plans that have simply gone stale.$$),
('ci-f-1428','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What sources of evidence do assessors typically pull from when collecting security process data, beyond direct hands-on observation?$$,$$Ticketing or workflow system exports, GRC platform dashboards, HR and training/LMS records, backup and DR software logs, and identity and access management (IAM) system reports.$$),
('ci-f-1429','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What is metric trending, and why does a single data point rarely tell the full story?$$,$$Plotting a metric's value across multiple periods to reveal its direction and rate of change. A single snapshot can't show whether a program is improving, stagnant, or worsening - only the trend over time reveals that.$$),
('ci-f-1430','cissp','core',6,'6.3',$$What is an escalation threshold on a security metric, and why is one defined in advance?$$,$$A predefined value that, once crossed (e.g., the patch backlog exceeding 5%), automatically triggers management notification or a predefined response, ensuring the metric drives action rather than being passively reported and ignored.$$),
-- 6.3 collect security process data: acronym
('ci-a-464','cissp','acronym',6,'6.3',$$MTTD$$,$$Mean Time To Detect - the average time between when a security incident occurs and when it is identified, a key operational metric collected to gauge detection capability.$$),
('ci-a-465','cissp','acronym',6,'6.3',$$MTTR$$,$$Mean Time To Respond (or Resolve) - the average time to contain and resolve an incident once detected, tracked alongside MTTD to measure overall response effectiveness.$$),
('ci-a-466','cissp','acronym',6,'6.3',$$GRC$$,$$Governance, Risk, and Compliance - the category of platforms and programs used to centralize policy, risk, and compliance evidence, often the source system security process data is pulled from.$$),
-- 6.3 collect security process data: feynman
('ci-y-294','cissp','feynman',6,'6.3',$$Explain to a manager why a "green" backup completion dashboard can still hide a serious problem.$$,$$Model answer: A backup dashboard showing green typically means "the backup job ran and reported success" - but that's a statement about the process, not about the data. The backup software can log success even if the resulting file is subtly corrupted, incomplete, or was pointed at the wrong data source, because the software isn't actually verifying the backup is usable, only that it finished running without an error code. The only way to really know a backup works is to periodically restore it somewhere and confirm the data comes back intact - which is a different, deliberate activity from just watching the job status. A green dashboard tells you the safety net exists; a successful restore test tells you the net will actually catch you.$$),
('ci-y-295','cissp','feynman',6,'6.3',$$Explain why disaster recovery test frequency itself deserves to be tracked as a metric, not just whether the most recent test passed.$$,$$Model answer: A DR plan is a description of an environment at a moment in time - it assumes specific servers, specific network paths, specific dependencies. Every day that passes after a test, the real environment quietly drifts away from that snapshot: new systems get added, old ones get decommissioned, configurations change. A DR test that passed flawlessly eighteen months ago tells you almost nothing about whether the plan would still work today, because the thing being tested has changed underneath it. Tracking how often tests actually happen - not just their outcome - catches the situation where a plan looks "validated" on paper but hasn't actually been exercised against current reality in far too long, which is itself a risk worth flagging even without a single failed test.$$),
-- 6.4 analyze test output and generate a report: core
('ci-f-1431','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What is CVSS, and what does its score communicate?$$,$$The Common Vulnerability Scoring System - a standardized numeric score (0-10) capturing a vulnerability's severity based on factors like exploitability and impact, used to prioritize remediation consistently across findings from different sources.$$),
('ci-f-1432','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$Why do risk-based severity ratings typically weigh business context, not just a vulnerability's raw technical score?$$,$$The same technical flaw poses very different real-world risk depending on exposure (internet-facing versus internally isolated) and asset criticality (a production payment system versus an unused test server), so context adjusts a raw technical score into an actionable, business-relevant priority.$$),
('ci-f-1433','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What should a remediation recommendation in a test report include, beyond simply naming the vulnerability?$$,$$A specific, actionable fix (e.g., a patch version or exact configuration change), the party responsible for implementing it, and an appropriate target timeline based on the finding's severity.$$),
('ci-f-1434','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What is a POA&M (Plan of Action and Milestones)?$$,$$A document tracking each identified finding along with its planned remediation steps, responsible owner, required resources, and target completion date - used to drive findings through to closure and demonstrate accountability over time.$$),
('ci-f-1435','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$How does a report's executive summary differ from its detailed technical findings section?$$,$$The executive summary condenses overall risk posture, top business impacts, and high-level trends into non-technical language for leadership decision-making. The technical section provides full evidence, reproduction steps, and remediation detail for the practitioners who will actually fix the issues.$$),
('ci-f-1436','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What does "tracking findings to closure" mean, and why is it necessary beyond simply issuing a report?$$,$$Following each finding through remediation and re-testing/validation that the fix actually works, then formally closing it. A report that is filed and never followed up on leaves the underlying vulnerabilities unaddressed despite technically being "known" to the organization.$$),
('ci-f-1437','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$Why is re-testing (validation) an essential final step after a reported finding is marked "remediated" by the system owner?$$,$$It confirms the fix genuinely resolves the vulnerability as claimed, rather than accepting an unverified self-report of completion - accepting the claim at face value risks leaving a false sense of security in place while the exposure persists.$$),
('ci-f-1438','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What is risk acceptance as an outcome of a reported finding, and who typically must approve it?$$,$$A formal, documented decision by management or the risk owner to knowingly accept a finding's residual risk without remediating it, typically requiring explicit sign-off since it is a deliberate risk-management choice rather than an oversight.$$),
('ci-f-1439','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$Why should the sections of a security assessment report read by executives avoid technical jargon?$$,$$Executives make resource-allocation and risk-acceptance decisions based on business impact and priority, not technical mechanics; jargon obscures the message being communicated and reduces the report's usefulness for the decisions it needs to inform.$$),
('ci-f-1440','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What is finding deduplication when compiling a report from multiple tools or sources?$$,$$Merging multiple raw alerts or findings that describe the same underlying root-cause vulnerability into a single consolidated finding, preventing the report from overstating the true number of issues or overwhelming the remediation team with redundant line items.$$),
('ci-f-1441','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What is a false negative in test-output analysis, and why is it more dangerous than a false positive?$$,$$A real vulnerability that testing failed to detect or report at all. It is more dangerous than a false positive because it creates unwarranted confidence that a system is secure when a genuine, unknown exposure actually remains.$$),
('ci-f-1442','cissp','core',6,'6.4',$$What information should accompany each finding to make it independently reproducible by a remediation team?$$,$$Clear step-by-step reproduction instructions, the specific affected asset or component, supporting evidence (such as a screenshot, request/response capture, or log excerpt), and the conditions under which the issue occurs.$$),
-- 6.4 analyze test output and generate a report: acronym
('ci-a-467','cissp','acronym',6,'6.4',$$CVSS$$,$$Common Vulnerability Scoring System - a standardized 0-10 severity score used to prioritize vulnerabilities based on their exploitability and impact.$$),
('ci-a-468','cissp','acronym',6,'6.4',$$POA&M$$,$$Plan of Action and Milestones - a tracking document listing each finding's remediation steps, owner, and target date, used to drive findings to closure.$$),
-- 6.4 analyze test output and generate a report: feynman
('ci-y-296','cissp','feynman',6,'6.4',$$Explain to a new analyst the difference between what belongs in a report's executive summary versus its detailed technical findings section.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine handing the exact same report to the CFO and to the engineer who has to fix the bug. The CFO doesn't need to know it's a reflected cross-site scripting flaw in a specific parameter - they need to know "this could let an attacker steal customer session data, it affects our highest-traffic public site, and fixing it will take about a week of engineering time." That's the executive summary: business impact, overall risk trend, and what decision is being asked of leadership, stripped of jargon. The engineer needs exactly the opposite: the precise vulnerable endpoint, the exact request that triggers it, screenshots or logs proving it, and specific remediation guidance like which encoding function to apply. Put the CFO's content in front of the engineer and they can't fix anything; put the engineer's content in front of the CFO and they'll skim past it without understanding what's actually at stake. A good report layers both audiences deliberately rather than picking one.$$),
('ci-y-297','cissp','feynman',6,'6.4',$$Explain why a finding marked "remediated" by the system owner should still be re-tested before it's closed.$$,$$Model answer: "Remediated" as reported by the person who owns the system is a claim, not proof. They might have fixed the specific example you showed them without addressing the underlying root cause, so the same flaw is still exploitable through a slightly different path. They might have made a change that looks right in isolation but doesn't actually take effect - a config file edited on the wrong server, a fix that didn't survive a later deployment, or a patch that installed but didn't fully apply. Or they might simply be mistaken about what "fixed" means for this particular issue. Re-testing replaces that claim with direct evidence: an assessor actually attempts the original exploit again and confirms it no longer works. Skipping that step means closing findings based on trust alone, which is exactly the gap that lets supposedly-fixed vulnerabilities quietly persist.$$),
-- 6.5 conduct or facilitate security audits: core
('ci-f-1443','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is a right-to-audit clause in a vendor contract?$$,$$A contractual provision giving the customer, or their designated auditor, the right to inspect or audit the vendor's security controls directly - often invoked when a vendor doesn't otherwise provide adequate independent assurance, such as a current SOC report.$$),
('ci-f-1444','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What are Complementary User Entity Controls (CUECs) in a SOC report?$$,$$Controls a SOC report explicitly identifies as the customer's (user entity's) own responsibility for the overall control objective to be met - for example, the customer must still manage its own user account provisioning even though the vendor secures the underlying platform.$$),
('ci-f-1445','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is a bridge letter (gap letter) in the context of SOC reports?$$,$$A letter from the service provider covering the period between the end of a SOC report's audit window and the present date, providing interim assurance that no material control changes occurred while a new full report is being prepared.$$),
('ci-f-1446','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is a QSA (Qualified Security Assessor)?$$,$$An individual certified by the PCI Security Standards Council and authorized to conduct formal PCI DSS compliance assessments for organizations that store, process, or transmit payment card data.$$),
('ci-f-1447','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is the difference between a periodic (point-in-time) audit and continuous auditing?$$,$$A periodic audit assesses controls at scheduled intervals, such as annually. Continuous auditing uses automated tooling to evaluate controls on an ongoing, near-real-time basis, surfacing control failures far faster than waiting for the next scheduled audit cycle.$$),
('ci-f-1448','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is audit sampling, and why do auditors use it instead of testing every single transaction?$$,$$Selecting a representative subset of a population - for example, 25 of 10,000 access changes - and applying statistical methods to draw defensible conclusions about the whole population, since exhaustively testing every single item is usually impractical.$$),
('ci-f-1449','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What are the general categories of audit opinion an auditor can issue?$$,$$Unqualified (clean): controls are fairly presented and operating effectively. Qualified: controls are effective except for specific noted exceptions. Adverse: controls are not effective, or the presentation is materially misstated.$$),
('ci-f-1450','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What role does an audit committee (or board-level oversight) play in the audit process?$$,$$It provides independent governance oversight of the audit function, receiving audit results directly rather than only through management, which helps ensure management cannot unduly narrow the audit's scope or quietly suppress unfavorable findings.$$),
('ci-f-1451','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What does SSAE 18 govern, and how does it relate to SOC reports?$$,$$SSAE 18 (Statement on Standards for Attestation Engagements No. 18) is the AICPA standard under which SOC 1 and SOC 2 examinations are performed in the United States, defining the requirements for evidence gathering and reporting that the auditor must follow.$$),
('ci-f-1452','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What is ISAE 3402, and how does it relate to SSAE 18?$$,$$The international attestation standard, issued by the IAASB, for service organization control reports - broadly the international counterpart to the U.S. SSAE 18 standard, and often referenced for multinational audit engagements.$$),
('ci-f-1453','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$Why must an auditor maintain independence from the system or control being evaluated?$$,$$An auditor who designed, implemented, or operates a control cannot objectively evaluate it. Both structural independence and the appearance of independence are required for the audit's conclusions to be credible and trusted by third parties relying on the report.$$),
('ci-f-1454','cissp','core',6,'6.5',$$What defines the scope of a security audit, and why must it be agreed upon before the audit begins?$$,$$The specific systems, controls, locations, and time period the audit will evaluate. Agreeing on scope upfront prevents later disputes about what the audit's conclusions actually do or don't cover, and about whether an area found to be weak was ever fairly in scope at all.$$),
-- 6.5 conduct or facilitate security audits: acronym
('ci-a-469','cissp','acronym',6,'6.5',$$QSA$$,$$Qualified Security Assessor - an individual certified by the PCI Security Standards Council to conduct formal PCI DSS compliance assessments.$$),
('ci-a-470','cissp','acronym',6,'6.5',$$SSAE$$,$$Statement on Standards for Attestation Engagements - the AICPA standard (currently No. 18) under which SOC 1 and SOC 2 audit reports are performed.$$),
('ci-a-471','cissp','acronym',6,'6.5',$$CUEC$$,$$Complementary User Entity Control - a control identified in a SOC report that the customer, not the service provider, is responsible for implementing.$$),
-- 6.5 conduct or facilitate security audits: feynman
('ci-y-298','cissp','feynman',6,'6.5',$$Explain to a colleague why a customer receiving a vendor's clean SOC 2 report still needs to implement the Complementary User Entity Controls listed in it.$$,$$Model answer: A SOC 2 report only certifies the controls the vendor itself actually operates - it can't certify controls that live entirely on the customer's side of the relationship, because the auditor never examined the customer's environment at all. Think of a cloud storage vendor: they can prove their data centers are physically secure and their platform enforces encryption, but they have no visibility into, and no control over, whether the customer configured strong MFA on the accounts that log into that platform. The CUEC list in the report is the vendor explicitly saying "our control objective only holds true if you also do these specific things on your end." Treating a clean SOC 2 report as if it means "everything is fully secured, nothing left for us to do" ignores that half of the shared control objective was never the vendor's to fulfill in the first place - skipping the CUECs leaves exactly the gap the report itself warned about.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 6.1 design and validate assessment/test strategies
('ci-q-1400','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$A security manager is documenting exactly which IP ranges, applications, and time window an upcoming assessment will cover, and explicitly listing the production billing database as excluded. What activity is being performed?$q$,$q$["Defining the assessment scope","Performing the penetration test itself","Writing the final assessment report","Conducting the post-assessment lessons-learned review"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Documenting the boundaries of what is included and excluded, before any testing activity occurs, is scope definition - a foundational planning step that prevents unauthorized testing of out-of-scope systems like the excluded billing database.$q$),
('ci-q-1401','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$An organization asks an independent CPA firm to formally evaluate its controls against a specific published standard and issue a certifiable opinion on whether those controls are effective. Which of the three general evaluation activities - assessment, audit, or review - does this best describe?$q$,$q$["An audit","An assessment","A review","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A formal, independent, evidence-based evaluation against a specific standard that produces a certifiable opinion is the defining characteristic of an audit. An assessment is broader and more flexible without necessarily following one fixed standard, and a review is a lighter-weight, less formal check.$q$),
('ci-q-1402','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$A regulator requires that an organization's control environment be evaluated by a party with no stake in the outcome, specifically to avoid any conflict of interest in the results. What type of assessor selection does this requirement point toward?$q$,$q$["A third-party assessor","The organization's own internal audit team exclusively","The system administrators who built the environment","No assessor is required if the policy documentation is thorough"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A requirement for objectivity free of conflict of interest is precisely what an independent third-party assessor provides, since they have no stake in how the results turn out - unlike internal staff evaluating systems they themselves built or maintain.$q$),
('ci-q-1403','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$Before a penetration test begins, the client and testing firm agree in writing on which subnets are in scope, that testing may only occur between 10 PM and 6 AM, and who to call immediately if a tester accidentally disrupts a service. What document captures these terms?$q$,$q$["Rules of Engagement (ROE)","A Service Level Agreement (SLA)","A Business Impact Analysis (BIA)","A non-disclosure agreement (NDA) alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Rules of Engagement is specifically the document that defines a test's authorized scope, timing windows, permitted techniques, and escalation contacts - exactly the terms described in this scenario.$q$),
('ci-q-1404','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$Why does a penetration tester need explicit written authorization from an accountable owner before beginning any testing activity, even if the intent is entirely benign?$q$,$q$["Without it, the tester's actions could be indistinguishable from a real attack, exposing them to legal risk and the organization to a false incident response", "Written authorization is only a courtesy and has no real legal or operational significance", "Verbal agreement from any employee is always legally sufficient", "Authorization is only needed for internal teams, never for external testers"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Without documented authorization from someone empowered to grant it, active testing activity could be legally indistinguishable from unauthorized intrusion, and defenders monitoring the environment have no way to know the activity is sanctioned, risking a wasted incident response.$q$),
('ci-q-1405','cissp',6,'6.1','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO factors that should most directly drive which systems get prioritized first when planning a security assessment.$q$,$q$["The business criticality and potential impact if the asset were compromised","Applicable regulatory or contractual requirements affecting that asset","Whichever system the assigned tester personally finds most interesting","Alphabetical order of the system's hostname"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Sound assessment planning prioritizes based on business impact and compliance/contractual obligations - factors tied to actual risk. A tester's personal preference or an arbitrary ordering scheme like hostname alphabetization have no relationship to risk and should not drive prioritization.$q$),
('ci-q-1406','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$A test plan states: "This engagement must determine whether an external attacker with no prior access can reach the cardholder data environment." What element of assessment planning does this statement represent?$q$,$q$["The assessment's success criteria / objective","The rules of engagement's emergency contact list","The final remediation recommendation section","The audit opinion"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A statement defining the concrete question the assessment is meant to answer is the success criteria or objective of the test plan, giving the exercise a measurable target rather than leaving it open-ended.$q$),
('ci-q-1407','cissp',6,'6.1','ordering',2,$q$Order these steps of designing and validating an assessment strategy, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Identify the assets and risk/compliance drivers that determine what needs assessment","Define the assessment's objectives and success criteria","Decide whether to use an internal team or engage a third-party assessor","Draft the rules of engagement and obtain written authorization from the asset owner","Schedule the assessment and establish its recurring cadence going forward"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Strategy design starts with understanding what actually needs assessing and why, which shapes clear objectives, which informs the choice of assessor, which is formalized into an authorized ROE, and is finally locked into an ongoing recurring schedule rather than treated as a single one-off event.$q$),
('ci-q-1408','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',3,$q$A payment processor's contract with card brands requires that its control environment be evaluated by an assessor with no reporting relationship to the team that built and operates those controls. The internal security team, however, built and operates every control in question. What is the most appropriate path forward?$q$,$q$["Engage a third-party assessor, since the internal team's lack of independence from the controls it built disqualifies it from satisfying this requirement","Have the internal security team perform a rigorous self-assessment instead, since they know the environment best","Skip the requirement since the internal team is highly skilled","Ask a different internal department unrelated to security to sign off on the same controls without any of their own review"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$When independence from the very team that built and operates the controls is contractually required, only a genuinely external third-party assessor can satisfy it - an internal team's deep familiarity with the environment doesn't substitute for the structural independence the requirement demands.$q$),
('ci-q-1409','cissp',6,'6.1','matching',2,$q$Match each assessment-planning term to what it defines.$q$,$q${"left":["Scope statement","Rules of Engagement (ROE)","Success criteria","Assessment cadence"],"right":["The documented schedule defining how often each type of assessment recurs","The specific goal or question the assessment is meant to prove or answer","The document defining authorized testing windows, techniques, and escalation contacts","The boundaries of which systems, networks, and time period an assessment covers"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[3,2,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$The scope statement defines the boundaries of what's covered. The ROE defines authorized windows, techniques, and contacts. Success criteria define the specific question the assessment must answer. Assessment cadence is the recurring schedule governing how often the assessment type repeats.$q$),
('ci-q-1410','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$Two departments both request a security assessment of the same new application, but neither has agreed in advance on which environment (staging or production) will be tested or over what date range. What planning gap does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["A missing, agreed-upon scope statement","A missing rules of engagement escalation contact","A missing audit opinion","A missing patch management schedule"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Disagreement over which environment and time period are actually being tested is exactly the ambiguity a clearly defined and agreed scope statement is meant to prevent before testing ever begins.$q$),
('ci-q-1411','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',1,$q$Which of the following is the primary reason a data owner's sign-off is typically required before an assessment of their system can proceed?$q$,$q$["It confirms an accountable party has accepted the defined scope, timing, and operational risk the testing may introduce", "It guarantees the assessment will find zero vulnerabilities", "It is only a formality with no real accountability attached", "It replaces the need for a written rules of engagement document"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Stakeholder sign-off exists to confirm that someone with real accountability for the asset has knowingly accepted the scope, schedule, and operational risk of the planned testing - it doesn't predict or guarantee any particular outcome.$q$),
-- 6.2 control testing
('ci-q-1412','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',1,$q$A vulnerability scanner is configured with valid administrator login credentials for each target host, allowing it to inspect installed software versions and internal configuration settings directly rather than only what's visible over the network. What type of scan is this?$q$,$q$["A credentialed (authenticated) scan","An uncredentialed (unauthenticated) scan","A synthetic transaction","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Providing valid login credentials so the scanner can inspect internal configuration and patch state directly is the defining trait of a credentialed scan, which is far more thorough than an uncredentialed scan limited to an outside network view.$q$),
('ci-q-1413','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$An automated tool bombards an application's file-upload feature with malformed, oversized, and randomly generated input, hoping to trigger a crash or memory-handling error that reveals an exploitable flaw. What technique is being used?$q$,$q$["Fuzzing","A credentialed vulnerability scan","A tabletop exercise","Static code review"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Feeding a target malformed, random, or unexpected input specifically to trigger crashes or memory-safety errors is the definition of fuzz testing, distinct from a structured scan or a manual code review.$q$),
('ci-q-1414','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',3,$q$A security tool runs an agent inside a QA environment's live application process while testers interact with it normally, combining the exact source-code line responsible for a flaw with evidence that the flaw is genuinely reachable at runtime. What type of testing is this?$q$,$q$["Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST)","Static Application Security Testing (SAST) only","A black-box penetration test","A tabletop exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Instrumenting a running application to combine source-level detail with real, observed runtime behavior is exactly what IAST does, distinguishing it from SAST (which never runs the code) and a black-box test (which has no source-code visibility at all).$q$),
('ci-q-1415','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',1,$q$During a scheduled exercise, one group attempts to breach an organization's defenses using real-world attacker techniques while the defenders have no advance notice the exercise is even occurring. What is this attacking group called?$q$,$q$["A red team","A blue team","A purple team","An audit committee"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The group simulating real-world attacker techniques, typically without the defenders' foreknowledge, is the red team - offense-focused, as distinct from the blue team defenders or a purple team's collaborative format.$q$),
('ci-q-1416','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$During an exercise, the attacking group explains each technique to the defensive team immediately before attempting it, and the defensive team reports in real time exactly what their tools did or didn't detect, so both sides can jointly close detection gaps. What is this collaborative exercise format called?$q$,$q$["A purple team exercise","A covert red team engagement","A SOC 2 Type II audit","A vulnerability scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Deliberately breaking the usual secrecy so attackers and defenders collaborate and share information in real time, with the explicit goal of improving detection together, is the defining trait of a purple team exercise.$q$),
('ci-q-1417','cissp',6,'6.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO reasons NIST SP 800-53A recommends combining Examine, Interview, and Test methods rather than relying on any single one alone.$q$,$q$["Triangulating evidence across methods can reveal discrepancies between what's documented, what staff describe, and what actually happens", "Using more than one method increases overall assurance in the assessment's conclusions", "A single method is always legally prohibited under NIST guidance", "Combining methods eliminates the need to define a scope beforehand"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Combining methods triangulates evidence, exposing gaps between documentation, personnel accounts, and observed reality, and this multi-source approach increases overall assurance. NIST guidance doesn't prohibit using a single method, and combining methods has no effect on the separate need to define scope.$q$),
('ci-q-1418','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$A QA team deliberately tries submitting the same discount coupon code multiple times in a single transaction, and separately attempts to manipulate a hidden price field before checkout, to confirm the application safely rejects both attempts. What type of testing is this?$q$,$q$["Misuse case testing","A credentialed vulnerability scan","A tabletop exercise","Static application security testing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Deliberately modeling how a user might abuse a feature - reusing a coupon or tampering with a price field - and verifying the system rejects that abuse is misuse case testing, distinct from testing only the intended, well-behaved use of the feature.$q$),
('ci-q-1419','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',1,$q$A SAST tool flags a function as vulnerable to a specific injection pattern, but manual investigation confirms the flagged input is always sanitized earlier in the code path and the flaw is not actually exploitable. What is this flagged item called?$q$,$q$["A false positive","A false negative","A zero-day vulnerability","A confirmed exploit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A finding reported as a weakness that investigation shows is not actually present or exploitable is a false positive - the opposite of a false negative, where a real issue goes undetected entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1420','cissp',6,'6.2','ordering',2,$q$Order these phases of a penetration test engagement, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Pre-engagement: agree on scope, rules of engagement, and authorization","Reconnaissance: gather information about the target","Vulnerability identification and exploitation: find and actively exploit weaknesses","Post-exploitation: determine the real business impact of what was achieved","Reporting: document findings, evidence, and recommendations for the client"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$A structured penetration test begins with agreeing terms before any technical activity, moves through gathering intelligence, then actively finding and exploiting weaknesses, then assessing what real impact was achievable post-exploitation, and finally documents everything for the client in a report.$q$),
('ci-q-1421','cissp',6,'6.2','matching',2,$q$Match each control-testing technique to its primary characteristic.$q$,$q${"left":["Credentialed (authenticated) scan","Fuzzing","IAST","Regression testing"],"right":["Re-testing a previously verified control after a change to confirm it still functions correctly","Feeding a target malformed or random input to trigger crashes and reveal flaws","Scanning a host using valid login credentials to inspect internal configuration and patch state","Instrumenting a running application to combine source-level detail with real runtime behavior"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,1,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A credentialed scan uses valid login credentials for deep internal inspection. Fuzzing feeds malformed input to trigger crashes. IAST instruments a running application to combine code-level and runtime detail. Regression testing re-verifies a previously confirmed control or fix after a subsequent change.$q$),
('ci-q-1422','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',1,$q$An assessor pulls the change-approval logs from a ticketing system to determine whether every production database change over the past quarter actually received documented sign-off before deployment, as required by policy. What control-testing activity is this?$q$,$q$["Log review","A penetration test","A tabletop exercise","A phishing simulation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Examining logs - here, change-approval records - to determine whether a control actually operated as designed is log review, providing objective, after-the-fact evidence distinct from an interview or a live exploitation attempt.$q$),
-- 6.3 collect security process data
('ci-q-1423','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$An analyst compares the list of currently active directory accounts against the current employee roster and flags several accounts belonging to people who left the company eight months ago but whose accounts were never disabled. What is this an example of collecting?$q$,$q$["Account management review data identifying stale/orphaned accounts","Backup verification data","Training completion data","Disaster recovery test data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Comparing active accounts against an authorized personnel roster to identify accounts with no legitimate remaining owner is exactly the data an account management review collects, surfacing stale or orphaned accounts.$q$),
('ci-q-1424','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$A backup system has reported "job completed successfully" every night for a year, but no one has ever attempted to actually restore a file from any of those backups. What critical piece of process data is missing to genuinely verify the backups work?$q$,$q$["The results of a periodic restore test proving the data is actually recoverable","A second copy of the same completion log stored offsite","A higher backup frequency, such as hourly instead of nightly","The vendor's marketing materials describing the backup software's features"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A job-success log only proves the backup process ran without an error; only an executed restore test provides genuine evidence that the resulting data can actually be recovered when it's needed.$q$),
('ci-q-1425','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A training platform reports that 94% of staff completed mandatory security awareness training by this quarter's deadline. What type of process data is this?$q$,$q$["Training completion data","Account management review data","Backup verification data","Disaster recovery test data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A percentage of staff who finished required training by a deadline is training completion data, one of the core categories of security process data collected for program oversight.$q$),
('ci-q-1426','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A security team tracks the percentage of employees who click a simulated phishing link in each quarterly campaign, and observes that figure has fallen from 22% to 9% over the past two years. What is being measured and tracked here?$q$,$q$["A phishing simulation click rate trend, used to gauge workforce susceptibility to social engineering over time","A Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for the email system","A backup verification success rate","An audit opinion"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tracking the percentage of users who click a simulated phishing link across successive campaigns is a phishing simulation click rate, and observing it over time reveals whether workforce susceptibility to social engineering is improving.$q$),
('ci-q-1427','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',2,$q$After a disaster recovery test, the team records that the failover completed in 3.5 hours against a 4-hour target, that two of twelve applications failed to come back online, and that the exercise was a full interruption test rather than a tabletop. What category of process data has been collected?$q$,$q$["Disaster recovery test data","Account management review data","Training completion data","CVSS severity scoring data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Recording whether a DR test met its RTO target, which systems did or didn't recover, and what type of test was performed is exactly the process data collected to demonstrate a DR plan's actual effectiveness.$q$),
('ci-q-1428','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$A DR plan passed its full-interruption test flawlessly two years ago, but no DR test of any kind has been conducted since. Why should this itself be flagged as a concerning metric, independent of that old test's result?$q$,$q$["The environment has likely drifted significantly since the last test, so a two-year-old pass provides little assurance the plan still works today", "A DR plan never needs to be re-tested once it has passed once successfully", "The lack of recent testing has no bearing on the plan's continued validity", "Full-interruption tests are the only type of DR test that ever needs to be tracked"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Infrastructure and dependencies drift continuously, so an old passing result provides shrinking assurance over time; tracking testing cadence, not just the last outcome, catches plans that have effectively gone stale without a failure ever being observed.$q$),
('ci-q-1429','cissp',6,'6.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO pieces of data an effective account management review should collect.$q$,$q$["Which active accounts have no corresponding authorized employee or business justification", "Which accounts hold privileged/administrative access", "The personal opinion of each account holder about whether they still need access", "The account holder's favorite productivity application"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Identifying accounts lacking a legitimate authorized owner and identifying which accounts hold privileged access are both core, objective data points an account review collects. A self-reported personal opinion or unrelated preference data provide no reliable evidence for the review.$q$),
('ci-q-1430','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$A review of privileged accounts collects the full list of administrator-level accounts along with the date each one last actually exercised its elevated privileges. Several accounts have not used their admin rights in over a year. What does this data most directly support identifying?$q$,$q$["Unnecessary standing privileged access that could be reduced or removed", "The organization's overall patch compliance rate", "Whether the organization's backups are restorable", "The current CVSS score of an open vulnerability"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Correlating privileged accounts against how recently their elevated rights were actually used surfaces standing access that isn't being exercised - a strong candidate for reduction under least-privilege principles.$q$),
('ci-q-1431','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$A dashboard shows a single month's snapshot: "failed login attempts: 340." Without additional context, what is the primary limitation of this single data point?$q$,$q$["It cannot show whether the figure is improving, worsening, or stable relative to prior periods, since trend requires multiple data points over time", "A single month's count is always inherently inaccurate and unusable", "It proves the organization has already been breached", "It automatically qualifies as a Key Risk Indicator regardless of any other context"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A single snapshot cannot reveal direction or rate of change - only plotting the metric across multiple periods (trending) shows whether the underlying situation is getting better, worse, or holding steady.$q$),
('ci-q-1432','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A security dashboard is configured so that if the percentage of unpatched critical vulnerabilities exceeds 5% of the fleet, an automatic notification is immediately sent to the CISO without waiting for the next scheduled report. What concept does this configuration illustrate?$q$,$q$["An escalation threshold defined in advance to ensure a metric drives timely action", "A false positive in vulnerability scanning", "A SOC 2 Type I report", "A right-to-audit clause"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A predefined value that, once crossed, automatically triggers notification or a defined response is an escalation threshold - it exists to make sure a metric actively drives action rather than sitting passively in a report no one reads until later.$q$),
('ci-q-1433','cissp',6,'6.3','ordering',2,$q$Order these steps of producing a quarterly security metrics report, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Pull raw source data from the relevant system of record (e.g., backup software, LMS, IAM)","Validate and normalize the data for accuracy and consistent formatting","Calculate the metric and compare it against its defined target or threshold","Report the metric and its trend to stakeholders, escalating if a threshold was breached"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Producing a reliable metric starts with pulling raw data from its actual source system, cleaning and normalizing that data so it's trustworthy, calculating the metric and comparing it to a target, and finally reporting the result (and any needed escalation) to stakeholders - skipping validation risks reporting numbers no one can trust.$q$),
('ci-q-1434','cissp',6,'6.3','matching',2,$q$Match each type of security process data to the description of what it actually captures.$q$,$q${"left":["Training completion data","Backup verification data","Account management review data","Disaster recovery test data"],"right":["Comparing the active account list against the current employee/role roster to flag stale access","Results of whether the failover met its target RTO/RPO and which systems recovered successfully","The percentage of staff who finished required awareness training by its deadline","Results of periodic attempts to actually restore data from a backup, not just job-success logs"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Training completion data is the percentage trained by deadline. Backup verification data comes from actually attempting a restore, not just a job-success log. Account review data compares active accounts against the authorized roster. DR test data captures whether RTO/RPO targets were met and which systems recovered.$q$),
('ci-q-1435','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',3,$q$Over the past year, an organization's Mean Time To Detect has improved from 48 hours to 6 hours, but its Mean Time To Respond has grown from 4 hours to 14 hours during the same period. What does this combination of metrics most likely indicate?$q$,$q$["Detection capability has improved significantly, but the response/containment process has become a new bottleneck once an incident is found", "Both detection and response have gotten worse overall", "The organization's metrics program is malfunctioning and the data should be discarded", "MTTD and MTTR always move in the same direction, so this combination is impossible"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Reading the two metrics together tells a specific story: incidents are now found far faster, but something in the response workflow - staffing, process, tooling - is slowing down containment once detection happens, making response the new area needing attention.$q$),
('ci-q-1436','cissp',6,'6.3','mcq',1,$q$A security team pulls its quarterly risk register updates, policy exception approvals, and control-testing evidence all from a single centralized platform used to manage governance, risk, and compliance activities. What type of platform is this?$q$,$q$["A GRC platform", "A SIEM", "A DLP gateway", "A vulnerability scanner"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A platform centralizing governance, risk, and compliance activity - risk registers, policy exceptions, and compliance evidence in one place - is a GRC platform, a common source system for the security process data collected during assessments.$q$),
-- 6.4 analyze test output and generate a report
('ci-q-1437','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',1,$q$A report lists dozens of findings, each with a numeric score from 0 to 10 that captures how easily the flaw could be exploited and how much damage it could cause, and the remediation team uses that score to decide what to fix first. What scoring system is being used?$q$,$q$["CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System)","A SOC 2 Type II opinion","A Recovery Point Objective (RPO)","A Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A standardized 0-10 score capturing exploitability and impact, used to prioritize remediation across findings, is CVSS - the industry-standard vulnerability severity scoring system.$q$),
('ci-q-1438','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',2,$q$Two findings carry an identical raw technical CVSS score, but the analyst rates one as Critical and the other as Low in the final report. The Critical-rated finding sits on an internet-facing production payment server; the Low-rated one sits on an isolated internal test server with no sensitive data. What explains the different final ratings despite the identical technical score?$q$,$q$["The final severity rating incorporates business context, such as exposure and asset criticality, adjusting the raw technical score into an actionable priority", "The analyst made an error, since identical CVSS scores must always receive identical final ratings", "CVSS scores are randomized and carry no consistent meaning", "Only internet-facing systems can ever receive a CVSS score at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Risk-based severity ratings deliberately weigh business context - exposure and asset criticality - on top of the raw technical score, since the same technical flaw poses very different real-world risk depending on where it sits.$q$),
('ci-q-1439','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',1,$q$A report entry names a vulnerability but gives no specific patch version, no assigned owner, and no target date for the fix. What is this remediation recommendation missing to be genuinely actionable?$q$,$q$["A specific fix, a responsible owner, and a target timeline based on severity", "A CVSS score, since severity scoring is unrelated to remediation recommendations", "The tester's personal signature", "A reference to an unrelated finding from a prior year's report"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An actionable remediation recommendation needs a concrete fix, someone accountable for implementing it, and a timeline appropriate to the finding's severity - without these, "here is a problem" gives the remediation team nothing to actually act on.$q$),
('ci-q-1440','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',1,$q$A team maintains a living document listing every open finding from the past year's assessments, along with each one's planned fix, the engineer responsible, and the date it's due to be resolved. What is this document called?$q$,$q$["A Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M)","A Rules of Engagement (ROE)","A bridge letter","An audit opinion letter"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A document tracking each finding's remediation steps, responsible owner, and target completion date is a POA&M, used specifically to drive findings through to closure and maintain accountability over time.$q$),
('ci-q-1441','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',1,$q$A CISO reading a security report wants to know the overall risk trend and top business impacts in plain language, without needing to understand the technical mechanics of any single vulnerability. Which report section is written specifically for this audience?$q$,$q$["The executive summary","The detailed technical findings section","The raw scanner output appendix","The rules of engagement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The executive summary condenses overall risk posture and business impact into non-technical language for leadership decision-making, distinct from the technical section's detailed, practitioner-focused evidence and remediation steps.$q$),
('ci-q-1442','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',3,$q$A critical finding from six months ago was formally documented and delivered in a report, but no one has since checked whether it was fixed, and the underlying vulnerability remains fully exploitable today. What process gap does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["A failure to track the finding through remediation to closure, leaving the report's existence a false sense of resolution", "The report itself was written incorrectly", "This is expected and requires no further action once a report is issued", "The finding should have been given a lower CVSS score"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Issuing a report is not the same as resolving the underlying risk; without tracking findings through remediation and verifying closure, a documented, known issue can remain exploitable indefinitely despite technically being "on the record."$q$),
('ci-q-1443','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',2,$q$A system owner tells the assessment team a critical finding has been "fixed" and requests the item be closed. Before closing it, the assessor attempts the original exploit again against the live system. What is this step called, and why is it performed?$q$,$q$["Re-testing (validation) - to confirm the fix genuinely resolves the issue rather than accepting an unverified claim", "A tabletop exercise - to discuss the fix informally", "A bridge letter - to cover a gap in audit coverage", "A CVSS rescoring - to lower the finding's severity automatically"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Re-testing directly verifies that a claimed fix actually works by attempting the original exploit again, replacing an unverified self-report with direct evidence before the finding is formally closed.$q$),
('ci-q-1444','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',3,$q$A finding's remediation would cost far more than the realistic financial impact of the risk it represents, and the business unit's vice president formally documents and signs a decision to leave the finding unremediated. What outcome does this represent?$q$,$q$["Risk acceptance - a deliberate, documented management decision to retain the residual risk without fixing it", "Risk transfer, since a signature always constitutes a transfer of risk to a third party", "A false positive, since management disagreed with the finding", "An automatic closure requiring no further tracking of any kind"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A formal, documented decision by an accountable owner to knowingly retain a finding's residual risk rather than remediate it is risk acceptance - a deliberate risk-management choice, not a dismissal of the finding as invalid.$q$),
('ci-q-1445','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',3,$q$A vulnerability scan reported a clean result for a particular web server, but months later an attacker exploits a flaw on that exact server that the scan should have caught. What does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["A false negative - a real vulnerability that testing failed to detect, creating unwarranted confidence in the system's security", "A false positive - a flaw wrongly reported as present", "A successful risk acceptance decision", "A properly functioning CVSS scoring process"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A real vulnerability that testing failed to detect at all is a false negative - more dangerous than a false positive because it leaves a genuine, unknown exposure while creating false confidence that the system was checked and found clean.$q$),
('ci-q-1446','cissp',6,'6.4','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO elements that make a reported finding independently reproducible and actionable for a remediation team.$q$,$q$["Clear step-by-step reproduction instructions for the issue", "Supporting evidence such as a screenshot, request/response capture, or log excerpt", "The tester's general career history and past certifications", "The total number of unrelated findings discovered elsewhere in the same report"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Reproduction steps and concrete supporting evidence let a remediation team independently confirm and understand the issue without relying on the original tester. The tester's career history and an unrelated finding count elsewhere in the report add nothing to reproducing this specific issue.$q$),
('ci-q-1447','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',3,$q$A report compiled from three different scanning tools initially lists 40 findings, but after analysis reveals that 12 of those are separate alerts describing the very same underlying misconfiguration, the analyst merges them into a single consolidated finding. What activity is this?$q$,$q$["Finding deduplication", "Risk acceptance", "A bridge letter process", "A red team exercise"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Merging multiple raw alerts that describe the same root-cause issue into one consolidated finding is deduplication, preventing the report from overstating the true number of distinct issues or burying the remediation team in redundant items.$q$),
('ci-q-1448','cissp',6,'6.4','ordering',2,$q$Order these steps of analyzing test output and generating a report, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Triage raw findings from all tools/sources, removing false positives and duplicates","Assign a risk-based severity/priority rating to each remaining finding","Draft a specific, actionable remediation recommendation for each finding","Tailor and write the report for both executive and technical audiences","Track each finding through remediation and re-test to confirm closure"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Analysis begins by cleaning raw findings of noise and duplicates, then assigning priority, then drafting concrete fixes, then writing the report itself for its intended audiences, and finally following each finding through to a verified close - skipping the final step leaves the whole exercise without lasting effect.$q$),
('ci-q-1449','cissp',6,'6.4','matching',2,$q$Match each report component to what it is or provides.$q$,$q${"left":["Executive summary","Technical finding detail","POA&M","CVSS score"],"right":["A numeric 0-10 rating used to prioritize a finding's severity based on exploitability and impact","A tracking document listing each finding's remediation steps, owner, and target date","A condensed, non-technical statement of overall risk posture and business impact for leadership","Full reproduction steps, evidence, and remediation guidance for the team fixing the issue"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$The executive summary condenses risk posture for leadership. Technical finding detail gives the remediation team full reproduction steps and evidence. The POA&M tracks remediation steps, owner, and date. CVSS provides the numeric severity score used for prioritization.$q$),
('ci-q-1450','cissp',6,'6.4','mcq',1,$q$A report rates one finding Critical and another Low, and the organization's policy requires Critical findings be remediated within 7 days while Low findings have 180 days. What is the primary purpose of assigning these different severity ratings?$q$,$q$["To drive proportionate, risk-based remediation timelines rather than treating every finding identically", "To determine which findings should simply be deleted from the report", "To decide which tester gets credit for the finding", "Severity ratings have no effect on remediation timelines in practice"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Severity ratings exist precisely to drive proportionate action - the most dangerous findings get the shortest fix windows, while lower-risk items are allowed more time, focusing limited remediation effort where it matters most.$q$),
-- 6.5 conduct or facilitate security audits
('ci-q-1451','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',1,$q$A cloud vendor declines to provide any independent report of its security controls, so the customer invokes a contractual provision allowing its own designated auditor to directly inspect the vendor's environment. What contractual provision made this possible?$q$,$q$["A right-to-audit clause", "A bridge letter", "A CUEC list", "An unqualified audit opinion"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A right-to-audit clause is the contractual provision that gives a customer, or their designated auditor, the ability to directly inspect a vendor's controls - typically invoked precisely when the vendor doesn't otherwise provide adequate independent assurance.$q$),
('ci-q-1452','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',3,$q$A vendor's SOC 2 report states that the customer is responsible for configuring multi-factor authentication on its own administrator accounts in order for the overall access-control objective to be met. If the customer never configures MFA, whose control failure does this represent?$q$,$q$["The customer's, since this is a Complementary User Entity Control (CUEC) explicitly assigned to them in the report", "The vendor's, since any control mentioned in a SOC report is automatically the vendor's responsibility", "No one's, since a clean SOC 2 report guarantees the entire objective is met regardless of customer action", "The auditor's, for failing to configure the customer's MFA directly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A CUEC is a control the SOC report explicitly identifies as the customer's own responsibility; if the customer fails to implement it, the resulting gap belongs to the customer, not the vendor or the auditor who documented the requirement.$q$),
('ci-q-1453','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',1,$q$A vendor's SOC 2 Type II report covers January through June, but a prospective customer needs assurance the controls are still in place through September while the next full report is being prepared. What can the vendor provide to cover this gap?$q$,$q$["A bridge letter", "A right-to-audit clause", "A QSA certification", "A POA&M"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A bridge letter specifically covers the period between the end of a SOC report's audit window and the current date, providing interim assurance that no material control changes occurred while a new report is being finalized.$q$),
('ci-q-1454','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',1,$q$An organization that processes payment card data needs a formal PCI DSS compliance assessment conducted by someone specifically certified by the PCI Security Standards Council to perform that assessment. What is this individual called?$q$,$q$["A QSA (Qualified Security Assessor)", "A DPO (Data Protection Officer)", "A CPO (Chief Privacy Officer)", "A red team lead"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A QSA is specifically certified by the PCI Security Standards Council to conduct formal PCI DSS compliance assessments - a distinct qualification from general privacy or offensive-security roles.$q$),
('ci-q-1455','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',1,$q$Rather than waiting for its annual audit, an organization deploys automated tooling that continuously evaluates key controls and flags failures within hours of occurring. What auditing approach does this represent, compared to a traditional scheduled audit?$q$,$q$["Continuous auditing", "A tabletop exercise", "A bridge letter process", "A qualified audit opinion"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Continuous auditing uses automated tools to evaluate controls on an ongoing, near-real-time basis, surfacing control failures far faster than a periodic, scheduled audit that only checks at fixed intervals.$q$),
('ci-q-1456','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',2,$q$An auditor reviewing 10,000 access-change tickets selects a random subset of 25 using statistical methods, then draws conclusions about the entire population's compliance based on that subset rather than examining every ticket individually. What practice is this?$q$,$q$["Audit sampling", "Continuous auditing", "A bridge letter", "Risk acceptance"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Selecting a statistically representative subset of a population to test, and drawing defensible conclusions about the whole from it, is audit sampling - used because exhaustively testing every single item is usually impractical.$q$),
('ci-q-1457','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',3,$q$An auditor finds that the vast majority of an organization's controls operated effectively throughout the period, but identifies two specific exceptions where a required approval step was skipped. The auditor's report notes these exceptions explicitly rather than declaring the whole control environment either fully effective or ineffective. What type of audit opinion is this?$q$,$q$["A qualified opinion", "An unqualified (clean) opinion", "An adverse opinion", "No opinion can be issued once any exception exists"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A qualified opinion states that controls are effective except for specific noted exceptions - distinct from an unqualified opinion (no exceptions) or an adverse opinion (controls are broadly not effective).$q$),
('ci-q-1458','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',3,$q$An organization's audit committee receives internal audit results directly from the audit function itself, rather than only through a summary filtered by the management team whose department was audited. What is the primary purpose of this reporting arrangement?$q$,$q$["To provide independent governance oversight and prevent management from narrowing scope or suppressing unfavorable findings", "To reduce the total cost of the audit function", "To eliminate the need for any written audit report", "To allow management to approve or reject findings before the board sees them"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Having audit results go directly to an audit committee or board, rather than being filtered through the audited management team first, is specifically meant to preserve independent oversight and prevent unfavorable findings from being quietly softened or suppressed.$q$),
('ci-q-1459','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',1,$q$A U.S.-based auditor performs a SOC 1 examination following the AICPA's requirements for evidence gathering and reporting on service organization controls. What standard governs how this examination must be performed?$q$,$q$["SSAE 18", "ISAE 3402", "PCI DSS", "GDPR"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSAE 18 (Statement on Standards for Attestation Engagements No. 18) is the AICPA standard governing how SOC 1 and SOC 2 examinations must be performed in the United States; ISAE 3402 is the broadly analogous international standard.$q$),
('ci-q-1460','cissp',6,'6.5','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO reasons auditor independence from the system or control being evaluated is essential to a credible audit.$q$,$q$["An auditor evaluating their own work cannot reliably self-review without bias", "Independence, including its appearance, is what makes an audit's conclusions trustworthy to third parties relying on the report", "Independence guarantees the audit will always find zero exceptions", "Independence removes any need to define the audit's scope in advance"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Independence matters because self-review is inherently biased, and because third parties relying on the report need to trust that the auditor had no stake in the outcome. Independence says nothing about whether exceptions will be found, and scope must still be defined regardless of who performs the audit.$q$),
('ci-q-1461','cissp',6,'6.5','mcq',2,$q$After an audit concludes, the audited business unit disputes whether a particular regional office was ever supposed to be covered by the engagement, since it was never explicitly discussed. What upfront step, had it been completed, would most directly have prevented this dispute?$q$,$q$["Agreeing on and documenting the audit's scope before the engagement began", "Issuing the audit opinion faster", "Using audit sampling instead of testing every item", "Hiring a QSA instead of a general auditor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A clearly documented, agreed-upon scope defined before the audit begins is exactly what prevents later disputes about which systems, locations, or business units the audit's conclusions do or don't actually cover.$q$),
('ci-q-1462','cissp',6,'6.5','ordering',2,$q$Order these steps of conducting a security audit, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Define the audit's scope and the criteria/standard it will be evaluated against","Gather evidence using a mix of examine, interview, and test methods","Evaluate the gathered evidence against the defined criteria","Form an audit opinion and report the findings","Follow up on remediation of any identified exceptions"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$An audit begins by fixing scope and criteria, then gathers evidence through multiple methods, evaluates that evidence against the agreed criteria, forms and reports a formal opinion, and finally follows up to confirm any exceptions get remediated rather than simply being left on record.$q$),
('ci-q-1463','cissp',6,'6.5','matching',2,$q$Match each audit-related term to its definition.$q$,$q${"left":["Bridge letter","Complementary User Entity Control (CUEC)","QSA","Audit opinion"],"right":["A determination such as unqualified, qualified, or adverse that an auditor issues based on the evidence gathered","An individual certified to conduct formal PCI DSS compliance assessments","A control identified in a SOC report that the customer, not the provider, must implement","Interim assurance covering the gap between the end of a SOC report's period and the present date"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[3,2,1,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A bridge letter covers the interim gap after a SOC report's period ends. A CUEC is a control the customer, not the provider, must implement. A QSA is certified to conduct PCI DSS assessments. An audit opinion is the auditor's formal determination based on gathered evidence.$q$);
